WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.420
My name is Alicia Williams,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.480
and I'm a gravestone conservator.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.653
Today I'm going to show you
how I clean gravestones.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.690
Before I start a clean,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.900
I take the time to assess the gravestone,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.550
and I make sure that it's not wobbly

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.220
or in any way deteriorating

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.673
before I approach the clean.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.940
The next step would be to apply a product

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.160
called D/2 Biological Solution.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.326
It's been tested and shown

00:00:37.326 --> 00:00:38.860
to be safe and effective

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.350
at removing all of the biological growth

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.730
on the surface of a marker,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.300
and it doesn't cause any harm

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.630
or leave behind any
residual damage or effects

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.770
like bleach would, or
regular household soap.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.210
I soak the whole marker,

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.852
and then I will allow that

00:00:53.852 --> 00:00:56.920
to dwell on the surface for a few minutes,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.623
and I will spray it down
with a little water.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.470
D/2 keeps working over time.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.010
That's how it's formulated.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.590
The warmer it is outside,
the better it works.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.440
I personally find the fall

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.550
is my favorite time to go clean,

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.020
because the D/2 works
throughout the winter.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.160
And so when you come back in March, April,

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.683
it's just an entirely new piece.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.290
This particular marker

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.560
had a significant amount of mossy growth

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.760
on the surface of it.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.080
It was a very satisfying scrape for me.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.551
I am scraping the lichen

00:01:35.551 --> 00:01:38.450
and surface debris off of this stone

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.966
before I begin the brushing process.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.610
Lichen is just little
mossy, flowery growths

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.040
that kind of stick onto the surface.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.360
They're harmful over time.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.553
They will completely overtake the stone.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.620
The type of grime and dirt
that's on a gravestone

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.220
varies depending on where you're located

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.440
and where the grave itself is.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.500
Is it under a tree?

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.640
Is it in the shade? Is it on a hill?

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.063
Does it get any sun?

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.340
It's mostly different
types of lichens and moss

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.930
and just dirt and grass and things

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.971
that get blown on it from mowing

00:02:20.971 --> 00:02:22.754
and caretaking efforts.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.720
I am using a regular bamboo skewer

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.880
that you would buy at
your local grocery store

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.663
to make sure I get all of the debris out.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.670
Occasionally I will pull those out

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.498
to facilitate the cleaning process.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.754
The main type of brush that I use

00:02:50.754 --> 00:02:52.350
is called a Tampico brush.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.730
It is made with natural bristles,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.040
and as you scrub the stone,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.230
the bristles just get softer.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.490
So it really limits any abrasion

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.246
to the surface of the marker.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.400
Some of the smaller ones that I use,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.333
the bristles will wear away

00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:11.108
instead of the stone itself.

00:03:11.108 --> 00:03:13.430
So I'll wear them down to almost nothing

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.525
and throw them away.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:18.610
Generally speaking,

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.430
if you wouldn't use the
brush to wash your car,

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.840
you don't want to use it on a gravestone.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.390
Anything harder than that

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.773
is going to possibly risk

00:03:27.773 --> 00:03:29.560
harming the integrity of the stone

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.446
or the inscription itself.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.840
I do not press hard. It's pretty gentle.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.360
If it's a newer, polished-granite marker,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.750
you can be a little bit
more aggressive with those,

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.740
because it's a stronger stone

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.110
and, typically, they're modern

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.140
and able to withstand a little bit more

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.996
than the older ones.

00:03:56.625 --> 00:03:59.290
Once I feel like I've
gotten the bulk of it off,

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.513
then I give it a good
rinse and let it dry.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.518
It's very important to keep water

00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:08.093
on the stone while you work,

00:04:08.093 --> 00:04:10.870
because keeping the stone wet as you scrub

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.710
limits any abrasion to the surface,

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.830
and it also helps whatever is on there

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.580
to come off a little bit more easy.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.600
I carry a lot of gallons of water with me,

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:23.850
a low-pressure sprayer --

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.710
that's important because
on historic stones,

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.632
they're a softer stone, marble, sandstone,

00:04:29.632 --> 00:04:32.330
and they're very easily
damaged by high pressure.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.050
So it's important to use
a low-pressure sprayer,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.520
like what you would
use to spray fertilizer

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.245
on your garden plants.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.826
The most important tip I have

00:04:45.826 --> 00:04:47.259
for someone who wants to get started

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:50.167
in cleaning gravestones is to remember

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:52.180
the main principle is to do no harm

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.196
and to ask permission

00:04:54.196 --> 00:04:56.920
before you work in any cemetery.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.370
The excitement of watching

00:04:58.370 --> 00:04:59.995
that first stone come clean

00:04:59.995 --> 00:05:03.993
was the first time I felt
a real, genuine smile

00:05:03.993 --> 00:05:07.140
come up on my face in a really long time.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.090
It just made me feel really good inside,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.113
like I had found a purpose.

